---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EvidenceLabel from '../components/EvidenceLabel.astro';
import TrajectoryTimeline from '../components/TrajectoryTimeline.astro';
import { offramps } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="Trajectory | Canada2080" description="A conditional Canada2080 trajectory through energy, compute, manufacturing, managed growth, resilience, and global contribution.">
  <section class="page-hero page-hero-dark"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Preferred future / Now–2080</p><h1>The long horizon starts with a working unit.</h1><p>Canada2080 turns theory into practice by proving capability at the smallest viable scale, connecting each link into regional systems, and scaling only what creates resilience, public value, and strategic choice.</p><EvidenceLabel label="Aspiration" /></div></section>
  <section class="section section-horizon"><div class="shell"><TrajectoryTimeline /></div></section>

  <section class="section shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">One framework, every scale</p><h2>Household. Community. Region. Nation.</h2></div><p>Recent Canada2080 working sessions sharpened the trajectory around scale. A national system becomes credible when its principles can be tested in a household, building, farm, hub, or community, then connected without creating one point of failure.</p></div><ol class="scale-ladder"><li><span>01</span><h3>Prove a unit</h3><p>Test energy, water, food, compute, or production with real users, operators, costs, rights, and recovery measures.</p></li><li><span>02</span><h3>Connect a community</h3><p>Let smaller units share surplus capacity while keeping sensitive data, essential services, and local control protected.</p></li><li><span>03</span><h3>Build regional loops</h3><p>Join infrastructure, demand, skills, suppliers, repair, and public institutions into repeatable operating systems.</p></li><li><span>04</span><h3>Network national capability</h3><p>Link differentiated regions through standards, trusted exchange, redundancy, and domestic decision rights.</p></li></ol></section>

  <section class="section shell split-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">The enabling chain</p><h2>Energy powers compute. Compute accelerates the next systems. Industry makes them durable.</h2></div><div class="reading-column"><p>Since industrialization, systems at scale have depended on electricity and power. AI now makes secure, affordable compute an additional substrate for research, services, and production.</p><p>Neither electricity nor compute creates sovereignty alone. Canada also needs connected grids, hardware, networks, cyber operations, water and heat management, pilots, buyers, standards, manufacturing learning, skilled people, repair capacity, and reinvestment.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai">IEA Energy and AI ↗</a></div></section>

  <section class="section shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Near and middle horizons</p><h2>Build options before the next technology junction.</h2></div><p>The 2030s may bring major shifts across AI, automation, biotechnology, semiconductors, climate adaptation, and quantum technologies. The strategic task is to preserve operating capacity while the stack changes.</p></div><ol class="numbered-actions"><li><strong>By 2030</strong><span>Publish baselines, choose complete-chain demonstrations, and prove the first governed working units with real demand.</span></li><li><strong>2030–2036</strong><span>Integrate regional energy, compute, water, manufacturing, housing, skills, and demand into measurable learning loops.</span></li><li><strong>2041–2046</strong><span>Deepen selected value chains, suppliers, repair, and standards while adopting emerging technology through independently measured utility.</span></li><li><strong>2046–2056</strong><span>Network climate-adapted regional systems into national production, public-service, and supply-chain resilience.</span></li><li><strong>2080</strong><span>Contribute trusted capabilities globally while transferring authority, knowledge, and renewal capacity to the next generation.</span></li></ol></section>

  <section class="section section-dark"><div class="shell split-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">The compounding loop</p><h2>Returns must build the next capability.</h2></div><div class="reading-column"><p>Every successful chain should return value to the system that produced it. Operating revenue, investment returns, procurement savings, and exported expertise can finance research, infrastructure, workforce development, supplier depth, repair, and climate adaptation.</p><p>This closes the loop between resources and resilience. Extraction without domestic learning is an exit. Production without repair is dependence. Growth without reinvestment is a temporary surge. Canada2080 advances when each generation of capability finances and teaches the next.</p><a class="text-link text-link-light" href="/natural-advantage">Explore complete Canadian value chains <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>

  <section class="section section-dark"><div class="shell split-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">Comparator, not template</p><h2>Taiwan shows what multi-decade institutional continuity can build.</h2></div><div class="reading-column"><p>Taiwan's semiconductor history has several valid clocks: ITRI was established in 1973, and TSMC in 1987. The resulting 53-year and 39-year histories are not a fixed completion schedule for Canada.</p><p>The transferable lesson is continuity across public research, technology transfer, pilot capability, specialist firms, suppliers, workforce, export customers, and repeated manufacturing learning. Canada's credible path is selective and allied, with possibilities in photonics, sensors, power electronics, compound semiconductors, design, packaging, testing, and trusted applications.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="section shell split-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">Technology option</p><h2>Quantum may mature. The timetable is not known.</h2></div><div class="reading-column"><EvidenceLabel label="Scenario" /><p>A 15-to-20-year quantum maturation window can guide option preservation, talent, sensing research, application discovery, and cryptographic transition. It cannot be presented as a forecast.</p><p>Canada should track reproducible logical-qubit demonstrations, application-relevant benchmarks, reliability, cost, tooling, and end-to-end utility relative to improving classical systems.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://ised-isde.canada.ca/site/quantum/en/national-quantum-strategy">Canada's National Quantum Strategy ↗</a></div></section>

  <section class="section section-horizon"><div class="shell split-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">Civilizational horizon</p><h2>Build in Canada. Contribute to the world.</h2></div><div class="reading-column"><EvidenceLabel label="Aspiration" /><p>Canada can become a leading contributor to the world's resilient future through complete capabilities in clean energy, water and climate adaptation, trusted AI infrastructure, advanced manufacturing, food and health systems, scientific collaboration, and institution-building.</p><p>A future Type I civilization is an orienting horizon for planetary cooperation, energy stewardship, and human flourishing. It is not a prediction that Canada or humanity reaches a Kardashev threshold by 2080.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="section section-dark"><div class="shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Offramps to avoid</p><h2>Ambition needs early warning signals.</h2></div><p>Canada2080 tracks failure paths alongside its preferred trajectory. Advocacy becomes credible when it states what would invalidate or redirect the plan.</p></div><div class="offramp-grid">{offramps.map(([title, summary], index) => <article><span>0{index + 1}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{summary}</p></article>)}</div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
